neutrino-mystery-1391b8f6.interpretation:
The formula describes a Type-I seesaw mechanism, relating the observed light neutrino mass, $m_{
u}$, to two other mass scales. $m_D$ represents a Dirac mass term, analogous to the masses of other Standard Model fermions, arising from the Yukawa coupling between the left-handed neutrino, a Higgs doublet, and a right-handed sterile neutrino. This term is expected to be roughly on the order of electroweak scale masses. $M_R$ is the mass of the hypothetical heavy right-handed (Majorana) neutrino. The formula illustrates that if $M_R$ is very large (e.g., at a grand unification scale), then $m_{
u}$ becomes proportionally very small. This inverse relationship is the core idea of the seesaw: a large $M_R$ 'pushes down' $m_{
u}$ to tiny values, while simultaneously predicting the existence of much heavier neutrino states.

neutrino-mystery-1391b8f6.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit where $M_R 	o \infty$ (infinitely heavy right-handed neutrinos), the light neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ approaches zero, meaning effectively neutrinos would be massless, consistent with the original Standard Model. Conversely, if $M_R$ were comparable to $m_D$ (e.g., at the electroweak scale), then $m_{
u}$ would also be around the electroweak scale, which contradicts experimental observations of very light neutrinos. If $m_D 	o 0$, then $m_{
u}$ also approaches zero, indicating that without mixing between left and right-handed states (or if the Higgs vacuum expectation value is zero), neutrinos would remain massless regardless of $M_R$. The validity of the seesaw approximation itself relies on the assumption that $M_R \gg m_D$.

